  "sport": "soccer",
  "title": "Counter in three passes or fewer",
  "description": "Six against six on about 50 × 40 metres. At one end is a full-size goal with the red team’s goalkeeper. At the other end are three cone goals, about five metres wide, along the end line. The red team scores by dribbling through one of the cone goals, the blue team in the big goal. Swap roles at half-time.\n\nBoth teams counter as soon as they win the ball. A goal scored with no more than three passes after winning the ball is worth three points, other goals one. Players count the passes out loud so everyone hears when the chance of counter points is over.\n\nVariation: two extra points if winning the ball and the first forward pass happen in the same touch. The diagram is not to scale.",
  "coachingPoints": [
    {
      "id": "cp1",
      "text": "Look forward before you win the ball – where is the nearest team-mate behind the opponents?"
    },
    {
      "id": "cp2",
      "text": "Play the first pass forward if you can, ideally in behind."
    },
    {
      "id": "cp3",
      "text": "Players without the ball sprint forward at once and spread wide, so the ball carrier has options."
    },
    {
      "id": "cp4",
      "text": "The team that loses the ball: the nearest player presses, the others run back the shortest way."
    }
  ],
  "locale": "en",
